# Database Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your actual values
# NEVER commit the .env file to git!

# ── DATABASE MODE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# true   → LOCAL in-memory database (data resets on restart, no internet needed)
# false  → REMOTE Supabase PostgreSQL with the connection string below
# This value overrides "UseInMemoryDatabase" in appsettings.Development.json.
UseInMemoryDatabase=false

# PostgreSQL Connection String for Supabase
# Format: Host=db.YOUR_PROJECT_ID.supabase.co;Database=postgres;Username=postgres;Password=YOUR_PASSWORD;Port=5432
ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection=Host=db.xcwzpttuawfyxclzfseu.supabase.co;Database=postgres;Username=postgres;Password=YOUR_PASSWORD_HERE;Port=5432

# ── PASSWORD RECOVERY EMAIL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS IS WHERE THE GMAIL CREDENTIALS GO (this file is the template; put the real
# values in .env, which is git-ignored, and in Koyeb environment variables/secret
# in production).
#
# The API sends recovery codes through its own mailbox's SMTP server. Short version of
# why (full record in plans/password-recovery-plan.md): we own no domain, and every
# transactional provider refuses to send to arbitrary recipients until you can publish
# SPF/DKIM records. Sending through the mailbox itself means Gmail signs the message as
# gmail.com, so SPF and DKIM genuinely pass.
#
# Use a DEDICATED THROWAWAY Gmail account. Never a personal mailbox: this password
# grants full mailbox access to anyone who reads it, so the blast radius should be an
# account you do not care about losing.
#
# Gmail only issues an app password once 2-Step Verification is on:
#   https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#
# WHICH TRANSPORT RUNS (see Program.cs): "use the mailbox you were given".
#   - Email__Smtp__* filled in  → real SMTP sends, in Development as well as production.
#   - Email__Smtp__* left blank → in Development the message is written to the log
#     instead (so the whole flow works with no mailbox); in production the API still
#     starts, the feature is unavailable, and the sender fails with a clear message
#     when a code is requested.
# Email__FromAddress MUST be the same address as Email__Smtp__User, or Gmail will send
# a message that fails SPF/DKIM alignment and gets quarantined.
#
# THE APP PASSWORD IS NOT YOUR GOOGLE PASSWORD AND NOT AN EMAIL ADDRESS.
# It is exactly 16 letters/digits, shown by Google in four groups ("abcd efgh ijkl mnop").
# Paste it WITHOUT the spaces. A wrong value here fails with a bare
# "5.7.0 Authentication Required" from Gmail, which is easy to misread as a code problem.
Email__Smtp__Host=smtp.gmail.com
Email__Smtp__Port=587
Email__Smtp__User=YOUR_DEDICATED_GMAIL_ADDRESS
Email__Smtp__Password=YOUR_16_CHARACTER_APP_PASSWORD_NO_SPACES
Email__FromAddress=YOUR_DEDICATED_GMAIL_ADDRESS
Email__FromName=Triple Triad
#
# NOTHING ARRIVED? The endpoint always answers the same way on purpose (it must never
# reveal whether an address has an account), so the backend log is the only place that
# tells you what actually happened:
#   - "Recovery email sent: ..."                  -> it left the API; check the inbox and spam
#   - "Password recovery email could not be sent." -> SMTP failed; the exception is logged
#   - neither line                                -> no send was attempted: the address
#                                                    belongs to no player, or a limit refused it
# Note that mail sent through smtp.gmail.com does NOT appear in the account's Sent folder,
# so an empty Sent folder proves nothing either way.

# Where a reset link points. Production overrides this in appsettings.Production.json.
# It must include the frontend's base path, which is set in triple-triad-v2/vite.config.ts.
# While testing locally against the remote database, keep this pointing at the local
# frontend, or the emailed link will send you to the deployed site instead.
App__FrontendBaseUrl=http://localhost:5173/triple-triad

# The recovery rate limits (per-account / per-address / global) have sensible defaults in
# appsettings.json and only need setting here to tighten them after an incident. See the
# plan §6 for why they are layered.
# PasswordReset__CodeTtlMinutes=15
# PasswordReset__PerAccountPerHour=3
# PasswordReset__PerAccountPerDay=5
# PasswordReset__PerIpPerHour=10
# PasswordReset__GlobalPerDay=200
# PasswordReset__ResetAttemptsPerIpPerHour=10
